60410 #edb/ref 1069 "q-81866" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060410 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060410 #edb/ref 1071 "Simplify the statement $¬(¬X∧¬Y)$ using De Morgan's laws." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060410 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6ad44d3b-4c29-563d-b808-2a30dfb14db0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060410 #edb/ref 1074 "De Morgan's laws state that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}¬(X∧Y)≡¬X∨¬Y, \\\\ ¬(X∨Y)≡¬X∧¬Y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can simplify the given statement as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}¬(¬X∧¬Y) & ≡¬(¬X)∨¬(¬Y) \\\\  & ≡X∨Y\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060565 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186060566 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060565 #edb/ref 1061 154012 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060565 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "395fc7fe-3175-579c-a510-019d03a347ea" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060565 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186060982 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060566 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186060567 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060566 #edb/ref 1061 154013 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060566 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "73c107a6-14f7-5621-8623-0041277016c2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060566 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186059696 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060567 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186060568 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060567 #edb/ref 1061 154016 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060567 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4ace0250-c3fc-5767-9fc3-6986b4f949f3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060567 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186059697 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060568 #edb/ref 1061 154019 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060568 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3496d4e6-81b6-582f-b540-a1b948281c36" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060568 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186059698 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060982 #edb/ref 1064 "**De Morgan's laws** for logic tell us how to distribute a negation over a conjunction or disjunction.\n\n- De Morgan's law for conjunctions states that\n\n$$\n\\neg ( A \\land B) \\equiv \\neg A \\lor \\neg B.\n$$\n\n- De Morgan's law for disjunctions states that\n\n$$\n\\neg ( A \\lor B) \\equiv \\neg A \\land \\neg B.\n$$\n\nIntuitively, you can think that the negation \"flips\" the conjunction into a disjunction, and vice versa.\n\nTo prove that these laws hold, we can construct truth tables:\n\n- The following truth table shows that $\\neg ( A \\land B) \\equiv \\neg A \\lor \\neg B{:}$\n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,B\\,$ | $\\, \\neg ( A \\land B) \\,$ | $\\, \\neg A \\lor \\neg B \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm F$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm F$ |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm T$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm T$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm T$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm T$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm T$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm T$ |\n\n- The following truth table shows that $\\neg ( A \\lor B) \\equiv \\neg A \\land \\neg B{:}$\n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,B\\,$ | $\\, \\neg ( A \\lor B) \\,$ | $\\, \\neg A \\land \\neg B \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm F$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm F$ |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm F$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm F$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm F$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm F$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm T$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm T$ |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060982 #edb/ref 1065 7973 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060982 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "11eb79a8-713f-5d33-bb4e-af361254bcc9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060982 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186376261 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186376262 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186376261 #edb/ref 1069 "q-182449" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186376261 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186376261 #edb/ref 1071 "$\\,$ $0$ is not positive and $0$ is not negative.\n\nWhat is the negation of the compound statement above?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186376261 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "631284c1-8510-54b7-a4ad-a5b7e737bce8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186376261 #edb/ref 1074 "De Morgan's law posits that the negation of a conjunction of two statements is equivalent to the disjunction of the negated individual statements. It can be stated symbolically as follows.\n\n$$\n¬(A∧B)=¬A∨¬B\n$$\n\nSo, the negation of our statement is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,¬(0\\text{ is not positive}\\text{ and }0\\text{ is not negative}) & ≡¬((0\\text{ is not positive})∧(0\\text{ is not negative})) \\\\  & ≡¬(0\\text{ is not positive})∨¬(0\\text{ is not negative}) \\\\  & ≡(0\\text{ is positive})∨(0\\text{ is negative}) \\\\  & ≡0\\text{ is positive}\\text{ or }0\\text{ is negative}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186376262 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186376263 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186376262 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186376264 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186376262 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186376265 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186376262 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186376266 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186376262 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186376267 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186376262 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186376262 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186376262 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186376267 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186376262 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "50e54abe-1f17-5c28-b368-9fe5216a089b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186376263 #edb/ref 1080 "$0$ is not positive or $0$ is not negative" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186376263 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186376263 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "32492387-ba7d-5d00-8da5-c3fafa28d652" #edb/ref 17